# AMPA Audit Result

---
## Goal

Fix the agent-reported error where agents invoking the /implement flow fail with "wl show --thread" missing. Determine whether the missing flag is a typo in callers or a missing CLI implementation (implement --thread or alias if needed), add tests and docs, and ensure agents can reliably call the command so threads/contexts are shown during automation.

## Instructions

- The work item for this bug is SA-0MKYE40VG1V2DTOX. Use `wl show <id> --children --json` to fetch the work item and its children when continuing investigation.
- Acceptance criteria (from the work item description) must be met:
  - Agents invoking the relevant path no longer log a missing-command error for "wl show --thread".
  - Implement the `--thread` option (or alias) on the `wl show` command, or update callers to use the correct existing flag; add CLI unit tests covering the behavior.
  - Add or update agent integration tests that exercise the flow in /implement so automation does not break.
  - Add a short note to developer docs describing `wl show --thread` usage and the /implement flow.
- When inspecting code, check both the engine adapters and the CLI implementation (argument parsing / subcommand registration). Also check agent code and delegated command invocations that call `wl show --thread` or run the /implement flow.

## Discoveries

- The work item SA-0MKYE40VG1V2DTOX exists and was fetched via wl:
  - Title: Agent error: 'wl show --thread' missing when running /impplement
  - Status: completed (stage: in_review), priority: high
  - Parent: SA-0MKYVWAKU14W795Z (Workflow)
  - No direct children and no inbound/outbound dependencies (wl dep list returned empty).
  - Description documents the problem, reproduction steps, suggested investigation, and acceptance criteria (implement flag or update callers; add tests/docs).
- I searched the repository for the literal "--thread" token. Matches were found almost entirely in runtime logs (.worklog) and scheduler artifacts — i.e., agent logs and scheduler history reference the error repeatedly, but I did not find an implementation of a `--thread` option in the code inspected.
- Many occurrences of `wl show` are present in the codebase, notably:
  - ampa/engine/adapters.py uses `wl show {id} --children --json` to fetch work-item data (we inspected this file).
  - Tests reference and mock `wl show` heavily (tests under tests/ and other engine/plan modules).
  - plan/wl_adapter.py and engine/core.py contain code that normalizes/parses `wl show` output and expects `wl show --children --json` output formats.
- No CLI argument parsing or `wl` command implementation file was found/read during this investigation (searches for add_parser/add_argument did not return CLI registration code in the repository subset searched). That suggests either:
  - The `wl` CLI implementation is outside the current repo path scanned (installed binary or different package), or
  - The subcommand/flag registration is in files not yet inspected; further search must target the CLI package/module implementing `wl`.
- No files were modified in this session; only reads and repository-wide searches + wl queries were executed.

## Accomplished

What was done so far:
- Ran `wl show SA-0MKYE40VG1V2DTOX --children --json` and captured the work item JSON.
- Inspected the work item details and acceptance criteria from the returned JSON / local worklog files.
- Searched the codebase and logs for occurrences of the literal "--thread" and for `wl show` usage:
  - Found many references in .worklog log files and scheduler_store.json (agent/scheduler logs show repeated triage selections referencing this issue).
  - Located `wl show` call sites in code that fetch work-item data (ampa/engine/adapters.py, engine/core.py, plan/wl_adapter.py) and in many tests that mock or assert `wl show`.
- Read ampa/engine/adapters.py to confirm how engine components call `wl` (it invokes `wl show {id} --children --json`), and to confirm there is no explicit handling of `--thread` in that adapter.
- Ran `wl dep list SA-0MKYE40VG1V2DTOX --json` — work item has no dependencies.

What remains / in progress:
- The root cause is not yet determined: no code implementing `wl show --thread` has been found in the inspected repository subset; we have not yet located the CLI implementation to confirm whether the flag exists or not.
- No CLI code was changed and no tests were added yet.
- Agent callers that invoked `wl show --thread` have been observed in logs, but the exact call-sites (which agent code invoked the flag) were not yet traced to source files.

## Relevant files / directories

Files inspected (read-only, no edits):
- Work item and logs:
  - .worklog/worklog-data.jsonl
  - .worklog/ampa/default/default.log
  - .worklog/ampa/scheduler_store.json
  - .worklog/logs/github_sync.log
  - .worklog/tmp-worktree-*/wt/.worklog/worklog-data.jsonl (temp worktrees)
- Work item queries executed:
  - wl show SA-0MKYE40VG1V2DTOX --children --json (output used to extract title/description/status/etc.)
  - wl dep list SA-0MKYE40VG1V2DTOX --json
- Engine code (key file inspected):
  - ampa/engine/adapters.py — confirmed how `wl show {id} --children --json` is invoked and how `wl` commands are wrapped by the engine adapter.
- Other code references (found by search; useful to inspect next):
  - ampa/engine/core.py — has logic around fetching/parsing `wl show` output (not fully read).
  - plan/wl_adapter.py — normalizes `wl show` outputs / comments parsing (referenced).
  - tests/* — many tests reference `wl show` and can be helpful to discover expected `wl show` output formats and any test fixtures for thread-related fields.

Directories to check next (likely relevant to CLI implementation and agent call-sites):
- (Search targets) repository root: look for CLI implementation — possible candidate locations:
  - cli/, scripts/, worklog/, src/worklog/, or a top-level package named worklog/wl.
- Agent code / skills that invoke /implement or `/impplement` flows:
  - skill/* and ampa/* directories (search for literal "wl show --thread", "show --thread", or "show --threads" again with deeper search).
- Tests referencing `--thread` or thread-related output models (search tests for "thread" token).

Suggested next actions (to continue the work)
- Locate the `wl` CLI implementation:
  - Grep repository for files that register `wl show` subcommand (look for "show" in CLI code, click/argparse usage, or package exposing "wl").
  - If not in-tree, run `which wl` on the working machine and inspect the installed `wl` binary or package source to determine whether `--thread` is supported.
- Search for the agent code that issues the failing invocation (grep the codebase for "impplement" and for exact call lines that build `wl show --thread`), and capture those call-sites to decide whether callers have a typo or are correct.
- Inspect CLI argument parsing to see whether `--thread` is absent and if there's an existing alternative flag that callers should use (e.g., `--threads`, `--thread-id`, `--context`), or implement an alias `--thread`.
- Add unit tests to cover `wl show --thread` behavior (or the chosen correct flag/alias) and add integration tests for the /implement flow per the acceptance criteria.
- Update developer docs (short note) describing the `wl show --thread` usage and the /implement flow once the final behavior is determined and implemented.
- Once the above is confirmed/implemented, update the work item SA-0MKYE40VG1V2DTOX with test/PR references and move to closed when acceptance criteria are satisfied.

Notes / context for the next agent
- The work item description explicitly leaves open the possibility that the failing invocation is a typo (e.g., agents calling `--thread` instead of the intended flag) or that the CLI lacks the feature — the next investigator must verify both caller code and CLI implementation.
- The engine adapters and many tests assume `wl show {id} --children --json` output; adding `--thread` should be compatible with that existing `wl show` behavior (it likely should be an optional filter/flag that extends show).
- Logs show this triage/issue surfaced repeatedly in scheduler runs; reproduce by running the agent automation that triggers the /implement flow (or run the code path that builds `wl show --thread`) to capture the exact failing shell invocation if necessary.
---
Continue if you have next steps, or stop and ask for clarification if you are unsure how to proceed.
